Ai : DeepSeek

## Abstract

We present a  rigorously grounded theoretical framework for a dynamic holographic information field—a complex-valued spatiotemporal scalar field \(\Psi(\mathbf{x},t)\) governed by an advection–diffusion–reaction equation whose source term embeds live data streams through a finite-distortion random projection. The choice of governing dynamics is justified by a systematic comparison of candidate partial differential equations against criteria of visual expressivity, stability, and computability. We prove well-posedness and energy decay for the coupled system, and we establish a constructive finite-distortion guarantee using the Johnson–Lindenstrauss lemma applied to a random linear map—not PCA—thereby correcting a prior mathematical error. For manifold learning methods such as UMAP, which lack comparable guarantees, the theorem serves as an idealization and motivates mandatory empirical distortion validation.

The computational pipeline translates the field into a viewable wavefront via a neural holographic synthesis network augmented by adaptive depth-layer allocation, temporal-coherence initialization, and optional phase-retrieval refinement. The velocity field construction \(\mathbf{u}=\nabla\times(\alpha\mathbf{p}\times\mathbf{x})\) reduces to uniform drift; we retain this minimal form for stability and document richer stream-function alternatives for future work. To address the documented vulnerability of Fourier Neural Operator surrogates to distributional shifts, we design a residual-based fallback that reverts to the full spectral solver when the FNO error exceeds a perceptually calibrated threshold. The optical bench is specified with component-level detail, including calibration protocols and a rotating-diffuser speckle mitigation strategy. A factorial within-subjects psychophysical evaluation is designed to disentangle the contributions of the field metaphor, stereoscopic depth, and true holographic cues, using a pre-test covariate adjustment instead of exclusionary criterion training to avoid selection bias. An incremental prototyping roadmap prioritizes testing the field metaphor on 2D screens and VR headsets before committing to the full holographic apparatus.

This dissertation is positioned within the design-science research paradigm. No physical prototype has been constructed; the work provides a corrected, complete, and theoretically sound blueprint for a novel information visualization medium.

