## Chapter 1: Introduction

The crisis of information perception—over \(2.5\times10^{18}\) bytes produced daily against a fixed human perceptual bandwidth—demands interfaces that transform abstract digital data into perceptually native forms. This dissertation introduces the concept of a **dynamic holographic information field**: a three-dimensional, time-evolving scalar wavefield whose amplitude and phase are directly driven by streaming data, optically reconstructed using computer-generated holography (CGH). The central thesis is that a carefully designed physical metaphor, rendered as a true volumetric image, can exploit the human visual system’s innate fluency with continuous flows, gradients, and depth to improve anomaly detection and spatial reasoning in high-dimensional data monitoring tasks.

We define a complex-valued field \(\Psi(\mathbf{x},t)=A(\mathbf{x},t)e^{i\phi(\mathbf{x},t)}\) over a bounded volume \(\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^3\), obeying an advection–diffusion–reaction (ADR) equation with a source term that encodes incoming data vectors. The choice of ADR dynamics is justified by a systematic evaluation of candidate PDE classes. The data embedding into the field parameters is achieved through a random linear projection that, by the Johnson–Lindenstrauss lemma, provably preserves pairwise distances up to a controlled distortion. This corrected foundation replaces an earlier erroneous claim that PCA provides a similar guarantee.

The computational holography pipeline is built around a neural tensor holography architecture with adaptive depth-layer placement, Gerchberg–Saxton refinement, and a speckle denoising module. To handle the computational cost of real-time field simulation, we propose a Fourier Neural Operator surrogate with a quantitative fallback mechanism that triggers full solver reversion when the surrogate error exceeds a perceptually justified bound. The optical bench is fully specified, including a phase-only spatial light modulator, single-frequency laser, and a rotating diffuser for optical speckle reduction.

A pre-registered perceptual evaluation design disentangles the metaphor effect (field vs. dashboard), the stereopsis effect (2D vs. stereo 3D), and the holographic depth effect (stereo vs. true holographic) using a covariate-adjusted within-subjects design that eliminates the selection bias of earlier criterion-training proposals. An incremental roadmap—2D field on a monitor, then VR, then holographic prototype—ensures that the core human-computer interaction hypothesis is validated before investing in the most challenging engineering.

This work is a design-science research artifact: a rigorously grounded blueprint whose mathematical, algorithmic, and perceptual components are fully specified and corrected, ready for staged implementation.

