## Chapter 3: Data Encoding and Psychophysical Calibration

### 3.1 Encoding Pipeline

Raw data are vectorized: text via Sentence-BERT, time series via short-time Fourier transform coefficients. The vector \(\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^m\) is mapped to \(\mathbf{p}=R\mathbf{v}\) using the pre-drawn random matrix \(R\). Field parameters are assigned:

- Velocity direction: \(\mathbf{u}=2\alpha\mathbf{p}\) (uniform drift).
- Source amplitude: \(A_S = \tanh(\|\mathbf{v}\|)\).
- Source phase: \(\phi_S = \operatorname{atan2}(p_2, p_1)\).
- Diffusivity: \(D = D_{\min} + (D_{\max}-D_{\min})\,\sigma(\text{entropy}(\mathbf{v}))\).

### 3.2 Psychophysical Calibration of Mapping Parameters

The mapping parameters \(\Theta=(\alpha,\beta,D_{\min},D_{\max},\ldots)\) are tuned using a perceptual distance matching procedure. A sparse set of representative data items is selected, and observers perform pairwise similarity judgments on field snapshots to obtain a perceptual distance matrix \(d_P\). \(\Theta\) is optimized via Bayesian adaptive direct search to minimize \(\sum_{i,j}(d_P(\Theta;\mathbf{v}_i,\mathbf{v}_j)-d_T(\mathbf{v}_i,\mathbf{v}_j))^2\), where \(d_T\) is a domain-appropriate semantic distance (Mahalanobis, dynamic time warping, etc.). A low-rank user-specific warp from a short individual calibration session further reduces intersubject variability.

