## Chapter 5: System Architecture and Technical Specifications

### 5.1 Optical Bench Design

The revised display volume is enlarged to **5 × 5 × 5 cm** at a 30 cm viewing distance, yielding a vergence angular difference of approximately 1.7° (102 arcmin) between the near and far planes—well above stereo discrimination thresholds and providing a robust depth cue. The optical bench comprises:

- **SLM:** HoloEye GAEA-2.1 (3840 × 2160, 3.74 µm pixel pitch, 60 Hz, 120 Hz overdrive capable).
- **Laser:** 532 nm single-frequency DPSS, 1.5 W, coherence length >50 m.
- **Optics:** Beam expander, spatial filter, half-wave plate/polarizer for phase-only modulation, Fourier lens (f = 200 mm), optional 4f relay with a rotating diffuser at the Fourier plane for speckle suppression, zero-order block.
- **Calibration:** Rapid stochastic method (Schroff et al., 2024) and reference-free regional calibration (Nam & Park, 2024) for per-pixel phase look-up tables; digital aberration map subtraction.
- **Safety:** Class 4 laser enclosure with interlocked access panels, beam tubes, laser safety eyewear (OD > 5 at 532 nm) for alignment, and thermal management via active SLM cooling and IR-reflective coatings.

### 5.2 Compute Infrastructure

A single NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24 GB) with an AMD EPYC 7543P CPU and 128 GB RAM. The software pipeline uses GPU ring buffers for inter-module communication, CUDA streams for overlap, and DirectX for SLM output at 4K@60 Hz.

### 5.3 Revised Latency Budget

| Stage | Component | Latency (ms) |
|-------|-----------|-------------|
| Data ingestion | Kafka/REST parser | 0.5 |
| Vectorization | Sentence-BERT / STFT | 1.5 |
| Dimensionality reduction | Random projection (matrix multiply) | 0.3 |
| Field simulation | Strang splitting on \(256^3\) grid | 5.0 |
| Layer allocation | Adaptive depth partitioning | 0.5 |
| Hologram generation | Neural forward pass + 2 GS iterations | 7.5 |
| SLM output | HDMI transfer + LC settling | 1.2 |
| **Total** | | **16.5** |

The 5 ms field update is justified by a detailed operation count: each time step performs two 3D FFTs (1 ms each), a semi-Lagrangian advection with uniform drift (effectively a constant-offset memory move with cubic interpolation, ∼1 ms), and a pointwise reaction (negligible). The total per-step time is approximately 3–4 ms; we budget 5 ms to include boundary handling and overhead. The FNO surrogate, when active, can reduce this to under 1 ms, but its use is conditional on a residual check.

