## Chapter 8: Limitations and Future Work

### 8.1 Corrected Mathematical Limitations

The finite-distortion guarantee of Theorem 2.4 applies only to the random linear projection path. When PCA or UMAP are used for exploration, the distortion must be measured empirically. The velocity field’s uniform drift may prove perceptually insufficient; richer constructions remain untested. The FNO fallback threshold requires perceptual calibration, which will be performed once the display is operational. Speckle reduction, despite the proposed optical and computational methods, may still limit image quality in the holographic modality.

### 8.2 Safety and Reliability

The optical bench involves a Class 4 laser; full safety interlocks, training protocols, and thermal management plans are mandated before construction. The SLM will be actively cooled, and optical surfaces will be monitored for dust-induced hot spots.

### 8.3 Future Work

Immediate steps: (1) implement the 2D field and dashboard and run the metaphor experiment; (2) if successful, develop the VR version; (3) construct the optical bench and calibrate the SLM; (4) execute the full perceptual evaluation; (5) explore richer velocity fields and color holography. Long-term, the integration of bidirectional gesture interaction and closed-loop cognitive-state adaptation could transform the field into a truly symbiotic information medium.

---

## 8.4 Concluding Remarks

This revised blueprint corrects the mathematical and perceptual flaws of the original proposal while preserving its vision. The use of random projections provides a genuine, constructive guarantee for data embedding; the depth volume is expanded to ensure a robust holographic depth cue; the experimental design avoids selection bias; and a staged implementation strategy de-risks the engineering. Together, these elements form a rigorous foundation for a novel visualization medium in which data becomes a tangible, living presence.

